<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=tunnelling-shield">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tunnelling shield</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tunnelling shield is a protective structure used while excavating tunnels through soft, liquid or otherwise unstable ground, supporting the face until a permanent lining can be built. The first successful one, a rectangular cast-iron frame moved by jackscrews, was developed by Marc Isambard Brunel and patented with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Lord Cochrane</a> in January 1818. Brunel and his son Isambard Kingdom Brunel used it to excavate the Thames Tunnel from 1825, though the tunnel did not open until 1843. James Henry Greathead first used a cylindrical shield at the Tower Subway under the Thames in 1869, and most shields are still loosely based on his design.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunnelling-shield.jpg" alt="Tunnelling shield" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thames_tunnel_shield.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bored with a <b>tunnelling shield</b>, it was the world's first tube railway, though it closed within months.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1818</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>London, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Marc Isambard Brunel · James Henry Greathead</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Brunel is said to have been inspired by the shell of the shipworm. Alfred Ely Beach had suggested a circular design in 1840 and built one in 1869, but Greathead is credited with the first cylindrical shield in use.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">Cast iron<span>8th century BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=tunnelling-shield">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
